y and exit rates at time $t.$ That is,\n\n$$\n{N}^{′}(t)=E(t)-L(t).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247285 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa38f112-466c-50e9-ac08-be72b6f53782" :knowledge-point/title "Relating a Derivative to a Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51117387-838c-5ae1-a569-486ef5d058e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(t)=-O(t)-C(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245033} {:answer/id #uuid "59f937e3-e9b5-5c9e-94e3-63a6aa799141" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(t)=O(t)+C(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245103} {:answer/id #uuid "911ba7f6-851c-5f58-a5fc-5068dd99b23c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{N}^{′}(t)=O(t)+C(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245583} {:answer/id #uuid "ea6976e0-a6e8-5f02-9bbb-665b9dd2e489" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{N}^{′}(t)=O(t)-C(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246393} {:answer/id #uuid "fe3ebe34-c337-5e89-b070-255a338cea0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(t)=O(t)-C(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea6976e0-a6e8-5f02-9bbb-665b9dd2e489" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{N}^{′}(t)=O(t)-C(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246393} :answer-field/id #uuid "5637c519-0dcb-5ebf-8eff-71f832658838" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246698}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a8187edc-1d98-57d1-8d80-0dd8b47814cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-112522" :question/problem "Oliver, the owner of a platform for chess players, uses the following three functions to model the daily number of users of the platform $t$ days after it was launched.\n\n- $N(t)$ - the number of users with an account\n\n- $O(t)$ - the rate at which new users open an account\n\n- $C(t)$ - the rate at which users cancel their account\n\nWhat is the relationship between the three functions above?" :question/worked-solution "The function $N(t)$ gives the number of users $t$ days after the platform is set up. Then,\n\n$$\n{N}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}N(t)}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }N}{\\text{Change in }t}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${N}^{′}(t)$ gives the rate at which the number of users with an account changes $t$ days after opening.\n\nIn addition, we can compute the rate of change of the number of users at time $t$ by finding the difference between the open and cancel rates at time $t.$ That is,\n\n$$\n{N}^{′}(t)=O(t)-C(t).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339060 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa38f112-466c-50e9-ac08-be72b6f53782" :knowledge-point/title "Relating a Derivative to a Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bd3314e-09b5-5ed6-b34d-9c8eb2cd078f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{′}(t)=r(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336291} {:answer/id #uuid "5f89b95a-dc2f-5355-a891-5f7ad0897f2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A(t)=-{r}^{′}(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336898} {:answer/id #uuid "d84adbce-e1e5-541f-ae42-248e49f85aef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{′}(t)=-r(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337909} {:answer/id #uuid "eb90804a-9cb1-5c0e-93ff-93893405ab87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A(t)={r}^{′}(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338088} {:answer/id #uuid "f843d514-c114-567f-8b4e-d0d7f91897e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A(t)=r(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bd3314e-09b5-5ed6-b34d-9c8eb2cd078f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{′}(t)=r(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336291} :answer-field/id #uuid "27c52aef-834f-5c1c-9709-b11a313c17d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4da77317-b047-59f4-8384-7a00ca57d0df" :question/math-academy-id "q-72105" :question/problem "Douglas, a pilot, is flying a plane. The plane's control systems record two measurements for different values of $t,$ where $t$ is the time, in minutes, since take off. The two measurements are:\n\n- $A(t)$ - the altitude (height) of the plane\n\n- $r(t)$ the rate at which the altitude is increasing\n\nWhat is the relationship between $A(t)$ and $r(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $A(t)$ gives the altitude of the plane after $t$ hours. Then,\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}A(t)}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }A}{\\text{Change in }t}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${A}^{′}(t)$ gives the rate at which the altitude is increasing at time $t$, which is the same as $r(t).$ Therefore, the relationship between $A(t)$ and $r(t)$ is ${A}^{′}(t)=r(t).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517311 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa38f112-466c-50e9-ac08-be72b6f53782" :knowledge-point/title "Relating a Derivative to a Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a07fcac-1eb0-55fb-b21b-22d00521717b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "E(t)=-a(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517308} {:answer/id #uuid "4402b726-403f-576b-b019-2b915e161c74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "E(t)={a}^{′}(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517309} {:answer/id #uuid "6356a13a-040d-5ec9-83bf-cd04457292a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "E(t)=a(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517310} {:answer/id #uuid "a419236d-aa04-5e6d-ac2a-931a0b50bd1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{E}^{′}(t)=-a(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517315} {:answer/id #uuid "c54787f5-85be-5ad0-abc1-60d1ab544ae9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{E}^{′}(t)=a(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a419236d-aa04-5e6d-ac2a-931a0b50bd1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{E}^{′}(t)=-a(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517315} :answer-field/id #uuid "19380a7d-eec1-50c7-88e2-68ca0c03ca17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6a01eb6f-a624-502a-a2a4-9a6dfc01e40c" :question/problem "A model records the mass of grain in a silo at time $t$, measured in hours after observations begin.\n\n- $E(t)$ - the mass of grain in a silo, in kilograms.\n\n- $a(t)$ - the positive rate at which that amount is decreasing, in kilograms per hour.\n\nWhat is the relationship between $E(t)$ and $a(t)$?" :question/worked-solution "The function $E(t)$ gives the mass of grain in a silo after $t$ hours. Then,\n\n$$\nE'(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}E(t)}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }E}{\\text{Change in }t}.\n$$\n\nThus, $E'(t)$ gives the instantaneous rate of change of that amount. The amount is decreasing, so the derivative is the negative of the positive loss rate $a(t)$. Therefore, the relationship is $E'(t)=-a(t)$.\n\nThe change ratio denotes an instantaneous rate: the limit of change ratios as the time interval tends to zero."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517312 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa38f112-466c-50e9-ac08-be72b6f53782" :knowledge-point/title "Relating a Derivative to a Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06f64cca-9d7b-5a4a-9e37-a59935ad93db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{E}^{′}(t)=-A(t)-L(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517306} {:answer/id #uuid "87998396-55ff-5827-af6b-d5ac4de36581" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{E}^{′}(t)=A(t)\\cdot L(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517313} {:answer/id #uuid "a8cefd14-af0a-577b-9ff6-5977036fbca0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{E}^{′}(t)=A(t)-L(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517316} {:answer/id #uuid "abd9afd1-5013-529b-a9a9-d0bf8b7e7f2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "E(t)=A(t)+L(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517317} {:answer/id #uuid "d81b1605-a414-59f1-952f-6ad51afd8862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{E}^{′}(t)=A(t)+L(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517319}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d81b1605-a414-59f1-952f-6ad51afd8862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{E}^{′}(t)=A(t)+L(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517319} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c8a937b-cd32-53bf-804a-42a180317817" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "73bef49f-6ef2-549c-9aa9-8a94dc95239f" :question/problem "A model records the mass of grain in a silo at time $t$, measured in hours after observations begin.\n\n- $E(t)$ - the mass of grain in a silo, in kilograms.\n\n- $A(t)$ - the positive rate at which the amount is added, in kilograms per hour.\n\n- $L(t)$ - the signed contribution of removal to the rate of change, in kilograms per hour; $L(t)<0$ when removal occurs.\n\nWhat is the relationship between the three functions above?" :question/worked-solution "The function $E(t)$ gives the mass of grain in a silo after $t$ hours. Then,\n\n$$\nE'(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}E(t)}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }E}{\\text{Change in }t}.\n$$\n\nThus, $E'(t)$ gives the instantaneous rate at which the amount changes at time $t$.\n\nIn addition, $L(t)$ already includes the negative sign of removal. We add the two signed contributions at time $t$. That is,\n\n$$\nE'(t)=A(t)+L(t).\n$$\n\nThe change ratio denotes an instantaneous rate: the limit of change ratios as the time interval tends to zero."}] :knowledge-point/title "Relating a Derivative to a Rate of Change"}]}
